                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you regarding your patient, a 73-year-old female, admitted on 18/06/1979 with acute lower abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding. Clinical examination, serum beta-hCG measurement, and transvaginal ultrasound confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy with signs of imminent rupture. Given her hemodynamic status and age-related risk factors, the decision was made for prompt surgical intervention. The patient underwent salpingectomy via laparotomy with successful removal of the ectopic pregnancy. Intraoperative findings confirmed a tubal gestation without peritoneal dissemination. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. She was discharged on 21/06/1979 in stable condition. Please ensure follow-up of beta-hCG levels to confirm resolution and monitor for any postoperative complications. Should you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me.</div>"
            },
